## Vicks VapoRub: What the 2011 Study Really Found

The most cited study on this option found a 27.8 percent complete clinical and mycological cure rate, an 83 percent positive treatment effect across the group, and 55.6 percent partial clearance among 18 participants over 48 weeks. These numbers come from one small pilot study, and the size matters as much as the result.

Published in the Journal of the American Board of Family Medicine in 2011, the trial followed 18 people with lab-confirmed onychomycosis applying Vicks VapoRub daily for nearly a year. Researchers tracked progress at six intervals using photographs and lab cultures rather than self-reported improvement, which gives the result more weight than most home-remedy studies manage. By week 48, five participants had achieved a true cure, meaning lab testing confirmed the fungus was gone. Ten more showed partial clearance. Three saw no change.

What likely drove the result is thymol, a compound in Vicks with documented antifungal properties, alongside camphor and eucalyptus oil. The <a href="https://www.jabfm.org/content/24/1/69">Journal of the American Board of Family Medicine pilot study</a> reported no adverse side effects across the full 48 weeks, making this one of the lower-risk options among <strong>home remedies for nail fungus</strong> even before factoring in effectiveness.

Eighteen participants and no control group means this study cannot carry the weight a larger randomised trial would. But a 27.8 percent cure rate confirmed by lab testing differs meaningfully from "people online say it helped." If a jar of Vicks is already in your cabinet, the data gives you a real reason to keep using it, with realistic odds attached.

