## Apple Cider Vinegar and Vinegar Soaks: Popular but Unproven

The question is not whether apple cider vinegar is safe enough to try. It is whether it clears an infection, and on that point the evidence runs out fast. Among <strong>home remedies for nail fungus</strong>, this is one of the most popular and least supported: no human clinical trials have tested apple cider vinegar against nail fungus directly. What exists is lab research showing acetic acid has some antifungal activity against certain organisms in a petri dish, plus a wide field of anecdotal reports.

That distinction matters more than it sounds. A compound killing fungus in a dish under controlled conditions says nothing about whether the same compound, diluted in a foot soak, can penetrate the nail plate the way it would need to for a cure. Acetic acid is acidic enough to create a less hospitable surface, which may explain some "it stopped spreading" reports, but a hostile surface and an eradicated infection underneath are different outcomes.

Most competitor content skips a real downside: undiluted or frequent vinegar soaks can irritate or chemically burn skin, particularly around a nail already compromised by infection. That risk deserves the same airtime as the remedy's popularity.

Dilute one part vinegar to two parts water rather than soaking undiluted, limit soaks to a few times a week, and watch for redness around the nail fold. Treat it as a low-cost experiment, not a treatment with the documented cure rates seen above.

