## Baking Soda, Hydrogen Peroxide, and Listerine: Where the Evidence Runs Thin

Baking soda, hydrogen peroxide, and Listerine sit in roughly the same category: each has a plausible antifungal mechanism in theory, none has clinical trial evidence showing it cures an established infection, and all three are popular largely because they already sit in most bathrooms. They belong near the bottom of any honest list of <strong>home remedies for nail fungus</strong>.

Baking soda is mildly alkaline and can help keep the area around an infected nail dry, since fungus favours moisture. Drying the surrounding skin is a reasonable supporting habit, not a documented treatment for the infection itself. Hydrogen peroxide shows antifungal activity in lab settings, and it visibly lightens nail discolouration on contact, which is exactly why people mistake it for progress. Lightening surface staining and clearing an infection are not the same event.

Listerine's reputation rests almost entirely on its essential oil content, thymol, eucalyptol, and menthol, the same family found in Vicks VapoRub. In theory that gives it some plausibility. In practice, no clinical trial has tested mouthwash soaks against onychomycosis the way the Vicks pilot study or tea tree oil trials were tested, so any claim about Listerine's cure rate is extrapolation, not data.

None of these three are dangerous in moderation, and none deserve ranking alongside tea tree oil or Vicks VapoRub, which have named studies and measured outcomes behind them. A hypothesis built on shared ingredients is reasonable. It is not evidence.

