## Nail Debridement: The Home Technique Most People Skip

Why does the one technique with real podiatric backing barely show up in roundups of <strong>home remedies for nail fungus</strong>? Debridement, thinning or filing down the infected portion of the nail, gets a fraction of the attention given to oils and soaks, despite addressing the exact penetration problem described earlier.

Debridement is the process of removing or thinning infected nail material to reduce fungal load and improve how well any treatment, topical or prescription, can reach the remaining infection. A nail file or dedicated debridement tool can gently reduce the thickness of the affected nail before applying any remedy. Less keratin between the surface and the infection means whatever you apply next has a shorter path to the fungus.

This is not cosmetic. Podiatric literature on topical antifungal treatment notes that debridement improves outcomes when paired with topical agents, because it addresses the nail-plate barrier rather than working around it. Urea-based debridement, using a 40 percent urea cream under occlusion to soften and remove infected tissue, has genuine podiatric support for symptom control, yet it is almost entirely absent from typical home-remedy content in favour of more natural-sounding options.

File gently and only on the thickened portion, never deep enough to cause pain, using a tool reserved exclusively for that nail. Pair it with whichever remedy you have chosen, and you address the mechanical obstacle rather than hoping a liquid finds its own way through.

